DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/artist-salon-north-african-tr
 aditions-new-music-creating-a-concerto-for-oud-and-baroque-orchestra\n\nJo
 in us for an Artist Salon: North African Traditions\, New Music Creating a
  Concerto for Oud and Baroque Orchestra\, a free public conversation prese
 nted by Philharmonia Baroque Orchestra & Chorale\, Stanford Live\, and Sta
 nford's Department of African and African American Studies.\n\nDiscover th
 e story of the oud—the fretless\, pear-shaped lute whose sound has shape
 d musical traditions across North Africa and the Middle East for centuries
  and which is an ancestor of the modern guitar.\n\nComposer Tarik O'Regan 
 and oud virtuoso Joseph Tawadros will take audiences behind the scenes of 
 a new piece O’Regan has composed for oud and Baroque orchestra\, schedul
 ed to premiere at Bing Concert Hall on February 7\, 2027.\n\nO'Regan\, who
 se music has been performed by leading ensembles around the world\, will d
 iscuss the inspiration behind the work\, while Tawadros—whose performanc
 es have introduced international audiences to the expressive sound of the 
 oud—will share the instrument's rich musical traditions\, demonstrate it
 s remarkable range\, and offer insights into bringing this new composition
  to life. The conversation will be moderated by Stanford professor and acc
 laimed scholar Professor Matthew D. Morrison\, whose work explores music\,
  culture\, and identity.\n\nThe event also features live musical demonstra
 tion by Joseph Tawadros\, a performance by the Stanford Middle Eastern Ens
 emble\, and an audience Q&A\, offering attendees the opportunity to engage
  directly with the artists.\n\nDate: Tuesday February 
